# PART 4 — VENDOR/SUPPLIER-BASED MATERIAL SELECTION AND PRICING

## 4.1 Supplier Integration

Integrate the Individual Items Catalog and Sets of Works directly with the existing Vendor/Supplier Management module.

A material may have multiple suppliers with different prices.

Example:

| Material | Supplier | Unit | Supplier Price |
|---|---|---|---:|
| CHB 4 inches | Supplier A | pcs | ₱18.00 |
| CHB 4 inches | Supplier B | pcs | ₱19.50 |
| CHB 4 inches | Supplier C | pcs | ₱17.75 |

These prices are illustrative only.

The estimator must be able to compare suppliers and select which supplier's material price to use in the quotation.

## 4.2 Supplier Material Price Records

Each supplier material record must support:

- Material or item.
- Supplier.
- Supplier item reference.
- Brand.
- Specifications.
- Unit of Measurement.
- Unit Purchase Price.
- Date of Price Update.
- Price Validity Date, if applicable.
- Supporting supplier quotation or price-list attachment.
- Remarks.
- Active or Inactive status.

Maintain historical supplier prices.

Do not overwrite historical prices when a supplier provides a new price.

## 4.3 Material Selection in Quotation Editor

Make material insertion fast and convenient.

Provide two methods.

**Option A — Search Existing Materials**

Allow the estimator to:

- Search by item name.
- Search by item code or SKU.
- Search by category.
- Search by supplier.
- Search by brand.
- Search by technical specification.

Display matching materials with available supplier prices.

**Option B — Quick Material Entry**

Allow the estimator to type the material name directly into the quotation editor.

Display matching materials as the user types.

Example: Typing "CHB 4" should display matching registered materials and available suppliers.

If no matching item exists, authorized users may create a new catalog item or quotation-specific custom item.

Prevent duplicate records caused by slightly different item descriptions.

## 4.4 Supplier Selection and Pricing

When a material is inserted into a quotation:

1. Retrieve its supplier price records.
2. Display available suppliers.
3. Display their corresponding prices and units.
4. Allow the estimator to select a supplier.
5. Insert the selected purchase price as the material cost input.
6. Apply the existing quotation pricing and gross profit rules.
7. Display the resulting selling price.

If no supplier price exists, flag the material as requiring price input or supplier quotation.

Never silently use zero or fabricated material prices.

## 4.5 Preserve Existing Quotation Pricing Logic

The existing quotation calculation engine must remain intact unless a separate approved calculation change is required.

Requirements:

- Supplier price is an internal purchase cost.
- The existing gross profit margin or pricing configuration must be applied correctly.
- Manual cost overrides require authorization and a reason.
- Save the selected supplier and supplier price snapshot in the quotation.
- Later supplier price changes must not modify existing quotations.
- Client-facing quotations must never expose supplier purchase prices, internal costs, or gross profit margins.

Maintain the distinction between gross profit margin and markup.

Do not change the meaning of existing pricing formulas.

